# Cases before suite execution\n\nEach new case was written before its first isolated invocation:\n\n```sh\ncargo test --locked --offline -p eventlog-sqlite --test atomic_blob_integrity_review CASE -- --exact --nocapture\n```\n\n| Case | Isolated result and exercised boundary |\n|---|---|\n| `corrupt_reuse_precedes_byte_conflict_and_all_guard_modes` | 1 passed, exit 0. A stored uppercase checksum plus unequal request bytes must return integrity Backend before byte conflict. No guard, nonreading guard and reading guard all preserve the complete before-state; neither tentative new bytes nor publication survives, and no guard is called. |\n| `malformed_sqlite_storage_classes_refuse_without_partial_publication` | 1 passed, exit 0. Text in byte_count, a blob in integrity_v1/hash, and text in bytes refuse through Backend without a panic or partial mixed-binding publication. Reading guard is never called. |\n| `retained_receipt_wins_before_corrupt_row_decode_after_head_advance` | Corrected setup: 1 passed, exit 0. After head advance, exact retry bypasses even malformed stored-row decoding and a reading/refusing guard, preserving original ranges/events. Changed bytes under the same key return IdempotencyMismatch first. Later erasure restores no binding. |\n| `foreign_corruption_and_guard_refusal_preserve_healthy_reuse` | 1 passed, exit 0. Corruption under another tenant does not contaminate own-tenant reuse, including an empty existing binding. A guard reads every tentative binding then refuses; all writes roll back. Retrying that uncommitted request with an allowing guard succeeds normally. |\n\nThe receipt case's first invocation exited 101 at setup because I cleared its advance request's blobs; the existing API correctly returned `Invalid(\"an atomic blob group must contain blobs\")`. This was **my invalid fixture, not an implementation finding**. `first-authored-test.rs` and the original first-run log retain it. I removed only that setup line, kept every assertion unchanged, and reran the exact case before the package suite; `corrected-retained-receipt.log` records its passing run. The other three first-run logs each contain one selected passing test, zero failed/ignored, three filtered out.\n\nThe synthetic SQLite inputs are generated by the retained test source. Each fixture database is deliberately retained under the assigned review TMPDIR, including first-run, corrected and package-run instances. Complete snapshots compare existing blob rows, events, per-command receipts and group receipts before/after refusal. No real data or operator store was used.\n\n## Suite and checks actually executed\n\nBaseline **112 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored** comes from the implementor's handback, not a pre-addition run.\n\n```sh\ncargo test --locked --offline -p eventlog-sqlite\n```\n\n`package.log`, exit **0**, contains 23 actual runner summaries totaling **116 passed, 0 failed, 0 ignored**. The new target runs all four cases. Existing atomic, corruption, callback, inspection, conformance, concurrency and native crash controls remain selected; no inherited case was weakened or skipped.\n\n```sh\ncargo clippy --locked --offline -p eventlog-sqlite --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo fmt --all --check\ngit diff --check\n```\n\nEach exits **0**, with separate logs and status files. Commands use two jobs/test threads, disabled incremental/debug output, direct rustc with an empty RUSTC_WRAPPER, offline/locked resolution, the assigned sequential target and private TMPDIR.\n\nDuring the package test run, available disk fell to 10,696,212,480 bytes, below the 10 GiB floor. Compilation had already completed. I stopped only my own orchestration shell so it could not proceed to Clippy while existing tests finished. After the coordinator reported recovered space, I rechecked the floor and resumed. Cargo's final exit was 0; Clippy then completed. No unrelated cache or temporary data was cleaned.\n\n## Source identity and assessment\n\nThese three delivered hashes are identical before and after this review:\n\n| Path | SHA-256 |\n|---|---|\n| `crates/eventlog-sqlite/src/atomic_group.rs` | `0bba7c302e79c1e9f4d984f3b60677ccdfdd2c23844d855c405667e4a68a8b0a` |\n| `crates/eventlog-sqlite/tests/atomic_blob.rs` | `b6c82d0c1045ea3dbb774e80ab35a54d413bec794279833ef654bd66b67d6083` |\n| `crates/eventlog-postgres/examples/production-proof/required.rs` | `204cce91eb91a692d8c2f04b94be94d34c1706a476137f4fc20ac059cbac779d` |\n\nThe published-base production file hashes to `1552aa2465ffb731cc35df5342bfc2afbb57a145488135cf97927f36e5f7ceba`. Its retained read-only copy and the scoped diff show the change: query the complete tuple and call the existing integrity validator before comparing bytes. Receipt lookup remains before this path; the native transaction still rolls back every tentative binding on refusal. No second validation algorithm or persisted format was introduced.\n\nThe new test file's final SHA-256 is `a0492af3814240192a0fc07fe6da5f9b6450922691c1731bb97396460ccb9e7c`. The six original release-probe source/manifest/script/log/status hashes match the retained release-probe review exactly and verify again afterward. That probe's source, lock, red output and databases were not modified or rerun.\n\nNo new concrete discrepancy was found. Executed cases support the bounded repair's integrity-before-publication and receipt-first compatibility requirements. This does not claim universal correctness or approve a release.\n\nOwners: the implementor owns the inherited source repair, tests and eight existing roster additions. I own four added cases, the corrected invalid setup and this report. The coordinator owns wave/changelog changes, roster inclusion for the new review cases, AEP, broader gates, CI and publication. No new finding is assigned to either implementor or coordinator.\n\n## Limits and handback\n\nThe PostgreSQL counterpart was inspected only: it already validates its complete tuple before conflict comparison. No live PostgreSQL service, production-proof run, full workspace gate, migration or consumer qualification was performed in this review. The original defective-release execution and implementor mutation runs remain attributed to their authors; I did not rerun a source mutant or alter the implementation.\n\nAll review-started commands have returned. No test/compiler process remains running. Only lease `codex-ekr-atomic-integrity-review` is released, with its result in `lease-release.log`. The new cases remain uncommitted for the coordinator; no automatic additional review is dispatched.\n\nExternal writes occupy three assigned roots: review scratch including retained synthetic databases/TMPDIR; the assigned Cargo target; and managed CLI lease bookkeeping. Exact host paths, environment and every retained scratch/database path are in private `private-inventory.md` and `retained-files.txt`. No worktree or evidence cleanup was performed.\n\n```findings\n[]\n```\n","relations":[{"reviews":"task:validate-atomic-blob-reuse"}],"status":"active","title":"SQLite atomic integrity independent review"},"lifecycle_state":"active"}},"id":"MIGa04b9bf5d0cc8b9f7a70418299134a95351e7a89c4574c62e8a811661c74794c","lifecycle_state":"recorded","revision":1,"version":1},"order":"per_kind_only","subject":["aep.entity","MIGa04b9bf5d0cc8b9f7a70418299134a95351e7a89c4574c62e8a811661c74794c"]}]